SM(558,{"n":"2 Corinthians 2-3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Corinthians 2-3.mp3","d":2595.6,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.2,"We're gonna get started in just a minute."],[23.12,"We'll be picking up St."],[25.68,"Paul's Second Epistle to the Corinthians, 2nd Corinthians chapter 1, verse 23."],[32.8,"We left off right at the end of chapter one."],[36.0,"Um because that's kind of the beginning of a new section."],[40.56,"Uh wherever the the chapter breaks."],[44.32,"We're not in here until the medieval period."],[47.52,"So they're sometimes um placed what looks like haphazardly."],[55.64,"Probably they ran out of the parch."],[57.8,"Well, yeah, it's it's hard to tell because of course the the earliest manuscripts we have are written in all capitals with no punctuation and no spaces between words."],[70.44,"Just written left to right and then right to left and then left to right, working their way down a page."],[76.84,"Um so even where we break paragraphs and sentences is a little arbitrary sometimes."],[83.24,"But sometimes when when you're reading when you're reading the text, we've seen obviously as we've been going through."],[89.48,"Sometimes where the paragraph or where the chapter break is makes perfect sense."],[94.46,"You look at it and you say, Oh, okay, well, yeah, this is starting a new section."],[98.62,"And then sometimes, like this, it's like, if you'd moved it back three verses, like it would make a lot more sense."],[104.38,"But these things are now standard"],[110.5,"So we'll be um, so we'll be picking up there right at the end of uh of chapter one."],[117.78,"And I'm not gonna spend too much time sort of recapping uh because uh we just had part of the first chapter."],[125.46,"And as usual, as we've talked about before, when St."],[127.7,"Paul begins one of his epistles uh in the introduction, uh the beginning chapter, he sort of lays out themes that he's then going to explore through the rest of the letter, and so um we'll be seeing these things come back up as we go, so there's no reason to sort of rehearse the summary again before uh we get started. digging in"],[151.9,"So that said, unless there's any comments or questions or anything left over from last time, we'll go ahead and get started in 2 Corinthians chapter 1, verse 23."],[170.86,"Moreover, I call God his witness against my soul that to spare you I came no more to Corinth."],[176.94,"Not that we have dominion over your faith, but our fellow workers for your joy, for by faith you stand."],[185.1,"So St. Paul was, and I'm gonna go back on what I just said and recap a little bit, but he has just been talking about the fact that he had planned to visit them, uh, not once but twice, and then obviously that visit hadn't worked out."],[200.68,"And so he's saying um that uh he did not come again to Corinth to spare them."],[210.44,"We talked about last time how his previous visit had sort of not gone well."],[215.56,"Um and so he didn't want, he thought it was probably not an opportune time yet for him to come back again."],[225.0,"Uh we talked about how part of the purpose of this letter is to try to sort of help resolve some of these situations and calm things down, both the problems they were having before, and then of course the things that happened in his visit that didn't go well."],[238.6,"And to smooth these things over"],[242.93,"So he's saying the reason he didn't come visit was not that he had something against them or something, or he was still mad at them or angry at them about how things went, but just that he thought it was not."],[255.49,"It would not be helpful to the situation for him to come again in person at this time yet."],[261.49,"And he calls God as his witness to that fact."],[263.91,"So he's just emphasizing look, I'm not just saying this to try to make everybody feel better."],[270.25,"This is true."],[271.15,"This is absolutely true."],[274.19,"He says, Not that I have dominion over your faith, but our fellow workers for your joy, for by faith you stand."],[280.83,"So he's saying he wants to be clear also."],[285.85,"That by saying that, by saying I I was gonna spare you, he's saying, you know, what that word dominion is sort of um it's related in English, but we don't always notice that it's related to domination."],[299.41,"So what St."],[300.21,"Paul is saying here is that he's not right there to come in and sort of force them into compliance."],[307.49,"We talked about before when we were going through Romans that the word that gets translated all the time as faith in English, really would be better understood as faithfulness, because it includes like loyalty and trust and these other things, not just sort of intellectual belief the way we normally think of of faith."],[327.21,"And so when he says that he doesn't have dominion over your faith."],[331.15,"He's saying, I don't I don't have dominion over your faithfulness."],[334.05,"So he's saying I'm not trying to like bring you into compliance right by force."],[338.93,"I'm gonna keep showing up and I'm gonna until you get in line."],[342.55,"That's not the approach he's taking."],[345.85,"He says, We, meaning he and Timothy, who's also sending this letter with him, he and Saint Timothy are fellow workers with them."],[355.34,"He says, We're all working toward the same thing."],[358.96,"We're all working, and and you you'll stand or fall, but you'll stand by your own faithfulness."],[366.94,"You need to be faithful."],[368.3,"I'm not going to come and try and you know force that on you, compel that."],[368.94,"Um, but I'm I'm working alongside you"],[376.56,"I'm striving to be faithful to Christ."],[378.8,"You're striving to be faithful to Christ."],[380.64,"He's giving them the benefit of the doubt."],[382.66,"That they're that they're making that effort."],[385.06,"And so we're all working together."],[386.66,"I'm not coming to lord it over you."],[392.82,"So chapter two, verse one."],[395.54,"But I determined this within myself, that I would not come again to you in sorrow."],[400.82,"For if I make you sorrowful, then who is he who makes me glad but the one who is made sorrowful by me?"],[409.46,"Now it seems to it's a little poetic."],[411.95,"But what St."],[412.51,"Paul is saying is he's repeating again what we just said."],[417.05,"He said, I don't I don't want to have come and have another bad visit."],[422.06,"Because he says, if I if I keep coming and having these bad visits, and I'm causing you sorrow and I'm causing you trouble and I'm causing more strife, then the person who I'm really going to be making sorrowful and causing trouble for is actually Christ."],[438.67,"And he said, Christ is the one who comforts me in my sorrows."],[442.25,"So I'm not in the business of going making other people sorrowful or causing other people trouble or other people suffering, or Christ's suffering by stirring up more trouble, with his people."],[452.98,"St. Paul's goal is to smooth these things over and resolve these problems, not to exacerbate them and have a fight, or even to win a fight if there already is a fight going on."],[465.98,"And I wrote this very thing to you, lest when I came I should have sorrow over those from whom I thought to have joy, having confidence in you all that my joy is the joy of you all."],[479.26,"So he says, you know, I I wrote to you before, before I came, and we read that."],[487.12,"First Corinthians. So I wrote to you before I came, and I was, and I told you at the time, you can go look it up."],[492.5,"That I was hoping when I came there would be joy."],[495.08,"There would be a good visit."],[496.73,"And uh, you know that that's what I was hoping."],[498.65,"Um but that's that, of course, is not what happened"],[506.21,"For out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote to you with many tears, not that you should be grieved, but that you might know the love which I have so abundantly for you."],[515.97,"Now, this is referring to that letter in between."],[519.7,"The so what's called the sorrowful letter that we talked about last time."],[523.78,"So he said, I wrote to you and I said, you know, that I was hoping it would be joyful, and that I wrote to you again, remember, and there was sorrow in that letter, but that sorrow wasn't that I was angry at you or that there was strife."],[537.52,"He's saying I was brokenhearted by how things went."],[540.9,"That it was it was difficult."],[542.34,"I didn't want to have to write that letter."],[544.72,"But that letter was coming from love."],[547.04,"That sadness was coming from love."],[551.52,"Verse 5, but if anyone has caused grief, he has not grieved me, but all of you to some extent, not to be too severe."],[562.0,"So he says, um if anyone has caused grief, he's talking about the people who were responsible for the problems, in the community."],[576.98,"Uh in 1 Corinthians, we talked about one of those."],[579.46,"There was one person who was living in sort of gross sexual immorality in the community, and they were all kind of ignoring it, even though they all knew about it."],[586.9,"And he was engaged in a relationship with his mother in law"],[594.62,"And everyone knew it was happening and no one was saying or doing anything and St."],[599.66,"Paul said no you need to take action."],[603.26,"But of course there were other problems and other people involved in those problems."],[606.7,"And so what St."],[607.34,"Paul is saying here is whoever there has caused problems for people."],[611.59,"They've hurt other members of the community."],[613.85,"He first says I'm not involved in this."],[617.79,"He said, I don't have anything against anybody."],[619.87,"Anybody who's, you know, I've so paul has forgiven anybody who said or did anything to him personally."],[628.0,"Um. And he's he's saying to them then in response, and by saying this, by saying, I don't have anything against anyone."],[639.42,"I've forgiven whatever everybody said to me, he's setting the standard."],[643.02,"What he then says to them, I don't want you to be too severe in terms of how you resolve these problems with each other."],[657.86,"So he says in verse 6, this punishment which was inflicted by the majority is sufficient for such a man."],[664.34,"So that on the contrary, you ought rather to forgive and comfort him, lest perhaps such a one be swallowed up with too much sorrow."],[672.98,"So the community, that's the majority that he's talking about."],[675.74,"The community had to take some kind of disciplinary action."],[681.82,"Well, we don't know for sure."],[683.1,"This probably includes it may include him."],[685.66,"This is more than one person."],[687.82,"But he's saying, so you you had to take some action and do some discipline."],[693.12,"And confront some people about some things."],[696.1,"And he's saying, what's been done is enough."],[701.67,"Don't continue like ostracizing these people."],[706.71,"Don't keep punishing them forever, you know, for what happened."],[711.51,"But you need to you need to forgive and be reconciled with them so that the what you're doing with them doesn't become too severe."],[717.11,"Because as we talked about in 1 Corinthians, when St"],[723.23,"Paul talks about taking these kinds of actions, they're the kind of things that we sometimes have to do in the church today with excommunication or you know, um, people can't receive the sacraments and that kind of thing, none of those are done as punishments."],[737.89,"It's not like, well, you did this thing and we're mad, so we're gonna punish you."],[742.3,"Or you hurt me, so I'm gonna hurt you back."],[745.74,"That's never why these things are done."],[747.42,"These things are done because we're trying to bring the person to repentance."],[753.26,"The goal is to bring them back."],[755.36,"And to reconcile them."],[757.16,"And if someone isn't willing to acknowledge certain problems and certain issues, sometimes you have to take some act to say, hey, come to your senses."],[765.98,"This is not acceptable, what you're doing, and what you're you need to, you need to make this right."],[771.18,"You know, but that's the goal."],[772.46,"The goal is that he would make it right."],[774.06,"It'd be restored."],[775.18,"And so St."],[775.82,"Paul's sort of re-emphasizing that."],[777.42,"He's saying, I know you're hurt, I know you're sorrowful, I know you've been grieved."],[781.84,"You're angry and hurt at what these people have done, but you can't sort of keep punishing them and keep taking it out on them forever."],[790.74,"Because that wasn't the point."],[792.83,"That wasn't the point."],[793.71,"The point was to correct things and restore peace."],[797.63,"And he uses this imagery of that person, if you keep punishing them, just being swallowed up by sorrow."],[805.57,"And never sort of recovering, which isn't what we want."],[811.89,"Verse 8, therefore I urge you to reaffirm your love to him."],[816.53,"For to this end I also wrote that I might put you to the test whether you are obedient in all things."],[823.09,"Now, whom you forgive anything, I also forgive."],[826.45,"For if indeed I have forgiven anything, I have forgiven that one for your sakes in the presence of Christ, lest Satan should take advantage of us, for we are not ignorant of his devices okay so he says anybody who you forgive and who you reconcile right he's reiterating again I'm not holding anything against anyone so if you forgive them and reconcile them Paul says as far as I'm concerned anything they said or did to me is forgiven right he says I've already forgiven that he's already forgiven them that in the presence of Christ meaning he's he's already prayed on the on their behalf right not just he said oh I forgive you but he's prayed to Christ that they would be forgiven and they would be restored."],[872.81,"He's actively praying for these people and sort of his final warning there is that is that this is something that Satan can take advantage of."],[884.63,"You may be legitimately hurt by what these people have done the these people have done to you."],[890.96,"And that legitimate hurt and pain is is legitimate hurt and pain and what that person did was wrong and this corrective action has been taken."],[900.16,"But the devil can come and take that hurt and pain of yours and use it to make you fall into sin if you start falling into bitterness and hatred and anger and revenge."],[911.3,"And wanting to get back at people."],[913.54,"He's saying you know you need to forgive for that reason as well right for that reason as well."],[921.7,"So you're not voiced right and and this is something this is something the fathers actually talk about a lot."],[928.82,"That if nothing else."],[934.04,"If nothing else, when we fail to forgive, we're torturing ourselves."],[940.12,"The person who we're angry and bitter at, you know, a lot of times doesn't know or care about how angry and bitter and hurt we are."],[950.65,"And so we're not taking it out on them."],[955.21,"We're mad at them and we're taking it out on ourselves."],[959.93,"We're sort of torturing ourselves, twisting up our own gut."],[963.07,"Living with all this bitterness and anger every day."],[966.11,"You know, having it destroy our own lives."],[969.95,"You know, when when forgiveness, we look at forgiveness like, well, if I forgive them, then I'm letting them off the hook for what they did."],[978.06,"But in reality, forgiveness is freeing ourselves."],[983.32,"For being sort of a slave to that passion of anger and bitterness and resentment."],[989.66,"Then we're free to move on with our lives in Christ and to change and grow and do better."],[996.14,"We're not sort of stuck in this place where we got stuck because we got hurt."],[1000.22,"I think we all know of people who don't know them personally, who something happened like that and bench fallen."],[1005.74,"they spent the rest of their lives being bitter and vengeful and Yeah, selves about it."],[1014.46,"Yeah. And so that's what St."],[1016.7,"Paul' warsning about here."],[1018.86,"It's not just for the sake of that person."],[1021.58,"Yes, he's already said we don't want to see that person get driven away from Christ, fall into despair."],[1022.62,"We don't want that"],[1028.34,"He says, but I also don't want to see you have the devil take advantage of this and use it to destroy you too."],[1036.92,"So there's a danger to everyone in the community from this if they don't pursue reconciliation now."],[1044.76,"Both sides. Not just the person who's the troublemaker or the bad guy."],[1049.88,"But both sides are in this danger."],[1057.16,"Verse 12. Furthermore, when I came to Troaz to preach Christ's gospel, and a door was opened to me by the Lord."],[1065.76,"Now we're getting back to his travel agenda about why he didn't come visit."],[1067.16,"We're still within that overall context"],[1073.2,"When I came to Troas to preach Christ's gospel, and a door was opened to me by the Lord, I had no rest in my spirit because I did not find Titus my brother."],[1082.24,"But taking my leave of them, I departed for Macedonia."],[1086.64,"So he's saying, in addition to me deciding that visiting you right then wasn't a good idea, I had this other opportunity, sort of surprisingly, come open to go and preach, preach the gospel in uh in Macedonia."],[1102.24,"This would be the Roman province of Macedonia."],[1104.3,"He says, so I, and we read about that actually in the book of Acts."],[1108.02,"But this is from St."],[1108.98,"Paul's perspective. So I had this opportunity too."],[1111.14,"So I thought on one hand, I had planned to go there, but on one hand, I thought now's probably that's not the best time to visit"],[1118.62,"And on the other hand, this opportunity to go and preach in Macedonia opened up, and so that's that's what he ended up doing."],[1127.02,"Why is he taking so much time to go through this?"],[1129.58,"Well, he wants them to know that he's not like avoiding them."],[1133.59,"Well, he was gonna come and visit us, and he didn't."],[1135.67,"We must have really upset him."],[1138.81,"He must be mad at us."],[1140.65,"Or he must be mad at those troublemakers, so we should keep beating them."],[1145.93,"Look, you drove Paul away, too."],[1149.85,"Another thing we can, you know, be angry about."],[1153.63,"So he's he's conc.erned to correct that impression to make sure they understand that it his not visiting, that's not why"],[1163.99,"Now, thanks be to God who always leads us in triumph in Christ, and through us diffuses the fragrance of his knowledge in every place."],[1173.19,"For we are to God the fragrance of Christ among those who are being saved and among those who are perishing."],[1180.31,"To the one we are the aroma of death leading to death, and to the other the aroma of life leading to life."],[1187.27,"And who is sufficient for these things?"],[1189.91,"For we are not, as so many, peddling the word of God, but as of sincerity, but as from God, we speak in the sight of God in Christ."],[1199.35,"The reason I read that big chunk is that this is actually uh St"],[1204.99,"Paul using an extended metaphor that isn't necessarily obvious to us, uh especially in English."],[1213.41,"But in our in our modern culture either."],[1215.81,"Because at verse 14 he says, thanks be to God who always leads us in triumph in Christ."],[1222.69,"So to us, we say, Oh, okay, well, yeah, we're victorious, in Christ."],[1227.18,"But a a triumph in the Roman Empire was a particular thing."],[1232.83,"A triumph uh was the closest thing we would have would be like a parade."],[1239.6,"Um probably the closest, closest thing we'd have would be like at the end of World War II when the soldiers came home and had the big victory parades."],[1248.22,"Like in New York and Washington."],[1250.62,"Um Roman triumphs were held for great generals for Caesar, you know, when some great had to this yeah this was this was an official honor."],[1263.26,"This wasn't just like hey I'm gonna celebrate me."],[1266.86,"This was an official sort of Roman honor that they would have this sort of this sort of victory parade after after some great victory or great accomplishment."],[1278.3,"I mean, it might be a literal military victory, it might be some kind of metaphorical right victory over a plague or over a you know some kind of other situation that the great man overcame."],[1291.44,"Um, some adversity."],[1293.72,"Um and uh so Saint St."],[1297.56,"Paul is this is not just a random metaphor either, because he's been talking about the gospel."],[1302.52,"And as we've talked about several times before in the Bible study, the word gospel, evangelion in uh Greek is was used in the plural uh in the Roman Empire, the evangelia, uh, which were uh read when a victorious general or uh Caesar or uh any major public figure was about to come and visit a city."],[1333.38,"Okay, they would come and read out the evangelia of whoever it was the senator, the general, the emperor."],[1341.3,"And that would be uh identifying who they are, and then a list of their great accomplishments."],[1348.68,"Here's the victories they won, here's all the things they've done."],[1352.2,"Uh here comes Caesar, he divided Gaul into three parts."],[1356.2,"You know, killed a million people and enslaved a million more."],[1359.48,"They bragged about stuff like that."],[1361.16,"Roman Empire Um so they they would come and read those."],[1366.44,"The idea being so that you would know who the great man was who was coming, so you could prepare to properly rece.ive him"],[1375.65,"That's the word that the New Testament picks to describe what St."],[1381.97,"Paul and the other apostles are doing is proclaiming the gospel, the evangelion, proclaiming the victory of Christ."],[1388.37,"So they're going from place to place, telling people who Christ is and and the great victory he had won over the powers of sin and death and and hell."],[1409.33,"I'm sure you've heard it."],[1410.69,"Yes. Is there anything is that just wrong, or is there anything to well that's that's uh woodenly literal."],[1417.81,"So that's you take the ev that means good"],[1424.1,"You take the Angelion that means news or a report."],[1428.68,"And the problem is that's not a really good way to interpret languages."],[1433.34,"It's to sort of take a word and pull it apart and then define the two parts."],[1437.42,"For example, what's a butterfly?"],[1443.03,"It's got nothing to do with a fly, and it's got nothing to do with butter."],[1446.99,"So you're gonna get confused."],[1449.43,"Yeah. If you try breaking apart that way."],[1452.61,"And so that's that's the problem with the good news thing."],[1456.13,"That's not like it's so far completely off kilter, but good news is pretty vague."],[1459.73,"I just saved a lot of money on my car insurance"],[1464.85,"That's good news."],[1467.89,"But that's not what we're talking about when we're when we're talking about Christ."],[1472.05,"Yeah. You sometimes get that just that vague sense."],[1476.21,"Um you also a lot of times uh our our Protestant friends, when they're talking about the what the gospel is, they're talking about how you get saved."],[1488.99,"The gospel is like if you've ever seen a four spiritual laws tracked."],[1492.76,"Or that kind of thing."],[1494.14,"It's like God loves you and has a wonderful plan for your life, sin has separated you from God."],[1498.46,"If you believe here's how you get saved."],[1501.04,"And the problem is that's about you."],[1504.74,"And what you need to do and like instructions."],[1508.23,"It's not about Christ."],[1512.31,"And just just the fact that St."],[1513.91,"Paul calls it the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[1516.31,"It's not like here's my self help program about how you can live a better life or not go to hell when you die."],[1524.08,"Or whatever else."],[1525.62,"It's it's about Christ."],[1527.7,"And the place where we really saw that that difference was in the book of Acts."],[1533.46,"Because in the book of Acts, every time St."],[1536.26,"Paul or one of the other apostles preached the gospel to people, their response pretty much every time was, What must I do to be saved?"],[1546.0,"Well, if that's the case, then the gospel can't have been what he needed to do to get saved, or he'd know and he wouldn't have to ask."],[1554.77,"So the gospel that's being preached by St."],[1557.17,"Paul is who Christ is and the victory he's won."],[1560.55,"Meaning he's talking about the incarnation, Christ's death, burial, resurrection, ascension into heaven, sitting at the right hand of God, and that he's who Christ is."],[1570.39,"to judge the living and the dead. He's coming"],[1572.71,"So here's who Christ is."],[1576.27,"What would your response be if you believed it?"],[1579.43,"Well, what do I need to do?"],[1581.44,"If he's coming to judge me, what do I need to do?"],[1584.93,"So the gospel isn't what you need to do to be saved."],[1588.33,"The response to hearing the gospel is, what do I need to do?"],[1593.47,"To be saved."],[1595.55,"And so that's what St."],[1596.91,"Paul has been talking about proclaiming so far here in 2 Corinthians."],[1602.91,"And so now he's sort of extending that metaphor here."],[1606.19,"He's just talked about going and preaching the gospel in Macedonia."],[1609.93,"And so he sort of takes that idea of the report of the victory and extends it to, well, Christ is, it's not just that I'm going and reporting this victory."],[1620.07,"It's that Christ is leading us continually in this triumph."],[1625.64,"In this sort of triumphal procession."],[1629.26,"This triumphal procession."],[1630.94,"Now, here's what's interesting about that."],[1633.42,"Because what St."],[1634.46,"Paul's been talking about in terms of what his life has been like recently for the last two chapters."],[1640.94,"Didn't sound super triumphant."],[1643.34,"There's a lot about sorrow, a lot about trouble, a lot about people being grieved."],[1648.06,"He's struggling with the community in Corinth, trying to help them solve all these problems."],[1652.22,"This is just one church."],[1653.76,"We've already seen it's got plenty of other epistles to other churches."],[1657.2,"They've all got problems too."],[1659.6,"Everywhere they go, there's all these problems."],[1661.44,"That doesn't sound like we're in this big triumphal march all around the world everywhere we go."],[1666.24,"And he's about to start making new one."],[1668.54,"Yeah. So it doesn't sound much like a victory parade."],[1673.18,"Paul is saying, no, that's what it is"],[1673.75,"And so St."],[1679.91,"Right? That's what it is."],[1682.07,"Just like to the people who didn't believe."],[1686.32,"Christ's crucifixion didn't look a lot like a victory."],[1691.45,"When Christ was dying on a Roman cross being tortured to death."],[1694.89,"That didn't look like winning."],[1697.71,"That was not the image of a winner."],[1701.13,"In their world."],[1702.49,"And so St."],[1703.45,"Paul is saying the same thing."],[1704.89,"I don't may not look like it, especially not to those who don't believe."],[1709.87,"But that's what this is."],[1712.84,"That's what this is."],[1714.04,"And that's not just important in terms of like, well, this is my mindset and this is what I imagine, so I can keep going through all this misery."],[1722.74,"No, he's telling them this because what's he been doing in everything we've read tonight so far?"],[1729.62,"He's trying to get them to look at these problems that they're dealing with from a different perspective than the one that they're in right now, which is reacting, reacting out of hurt and pain and sorrow."],[1745.83,"And he's saying, and he's been telling them no."],[1748.57,"You need to forgive, you need to be reconciled, you need to show love."],[1753.66,"And so on a more overarching level, you need to stop looking at this as just this horrible suffering."],[1757.42,"And misery He's already talked about sharing in the sufferings of Christ in the first chapter."],[1766.89,"And you need to start looking at this in the same way you look at Christ's crucifixion."],[1773.07,"That this is really a victory."],[1775.79,"And me getting run out of town after town and half stoned to death and beating and flogged."],[1780.43,"This is the vic this is a victory march."],[1790.27,"Celebrating his victory."],[1793.23,"Right in this in this triumphal procession, even if it doesn't look like it to outsiders all the time."],[1801.83,"And this is then the as he extends the metaphor."],[1807.21,"He talks about the fragrance of his knowledge at every place."],[1811.05,"So this is the report of the gospel."],[1814.35,"The knowledge of Christ."],[1819.43,"And so he's making a comparison when he talks about that fragrance of that to incense."],[1825.81,"Which would be used in these processions."],[1829.03,"There would be incense offerings going on."],[1831.85,"And so as he travels to all these places, the knowledge of Christ is coming out sort of like incense."],[1840.57,"Since for we are to God the fragrance of Christ among those who are being saved and among those who are perishing"],[1847.17,"So this as we come into town with this procession and the knowledge of Christ spreads."],[1852.9,"That fragrance is smelled, the analogy, both by those who are being saved and those who are perishing."],[1862.82,"The people who are believing believing and being faithful and coming to follow Christ, and those who are not, who are rejecting, who are rejecting that knowledge when they receive it."],[1872.9,"To the one, we are the aroma of death leading to death, and to the other, the aroma of life leading to life."],[1879.7,"Okay So. You you would have in these processions, you would have two kinds of people. in a tri in a Roman triumph participating"],[1892.31,"You'd have participants like the fellow soldiers of the commander."],[1897.05,"And the people celebrating."],[1899.35,"And so that aroma, that fragrance, that's coming out of the incense, that'd be the aroma of life and victory."],[1906.15,"So to those who are who are followers of Christ, who are co-workers."],[1914.12,"This is the fragrance they hear the gospel and it's this beautiful smell."],[1918.22,"It's a fragrance of life."],[1920.14,"The other people who would be in a Roman triumph would be captured prisoners and slaves."],[1926.86,"And to them, that smell was not a good smell"],[1928.3,"that smell Because meant, you know, at the end of this procession."],[1937.34,"That was the end of them."],[1938.92,"These captured prisoners."],[1940.52,"Or you get paraded around and then you know, done in."],[1944.76,"And so uh that reference would be though to those who don't believe, who reject it."],[1953.57,"Who all they see is the shame of the crucifixion, the shame of St."],[1959.81,"Paul. Because they don't believe."],[1962.07,"It's a foul smell."],[1964.01,"To them. The knowledge of Christ."],[1966.01,"And they reject it because it's just and it's just it becomes just death to them."],[1971.83,"It's the same message, the same report, but based on how it's received by the people, it becomes either life or death."],[1983.15,"And the the other the other reference here to have in mind, in terms of the scriptures, within this sort of Roman cultural analogy."],[1992.75,"Remember, in court, they're mostly Gentiles."],[2002.55,"God says, Behold, I set before you today life and death, blessings and curses."],[2007.93,"And those were going to be based on."],[2010.01,"That comes right at the end of the Torah."],[2013.53,"And so Yahweh, the God of Israel, is saying, I've given you my Torah, I've given you my law, and now, depending on what you choose to do with it."],[2021.75,"How you choose to receive it, it's going to mean either life or death for you."],[2025.67,"Either blessings or curses for you."],[2028.23,"And so same thing with the gospel as St."],[2031.59,"Paul proclaims it."],[2033.27,"Depending on how someone receives it, it's either going to be life to them or death to them."],[2041.51,"And so I'm saying, who is sufficient for these things?"],[2044.49,"So he St."],[2045.45,"Paul's making it clear he's not bragging."],[2048.15,"It's not his triumph."],[2050.07,"It's Christ's. He's like, I'm, you know, he's putting out he's not worthy to be the one doing this."],[2056.43,"But that this is what that's going to do"],[2062.05,"He says, we're not as so many peddling the word of God."],[2066.77,"But as of sincerity, but as from God, we speak in the sight of God in Christ."],[2070.61,"So he's saying, I'm not doing this to make money."],[2074.37,"Who is referring to these peddling?"],[2076.93,"Well, he has you referred to in 1 Corinthians, too."],[2080.45,"Remember, he went on about how, remember, I supported myself with my own hands."],[2085.83,"So there are apparently, I mean, we saw in the book of Acts, like Simon Magus come and try and buy the ability to give people the Holy Spirit from them."],[2095.03,"From the apostles, you know, teach me this technique."],[2097.87,"Um. that was not uncommon in the ancient world"],[2103.71,"I mean, that's how philosophers and teachers and stuff and rabbis even made a living."],[2109.41,"They would come and I mean a rabbi would go to a a place with a synagogue and stay there, and they would support him and he would sort of preach until he moved on."],[2119.03,"So that would be and same thing with philosophers, same thing with any other kind of teacher."],[2124.56,"They would go from place to place and sort of be paid to to apply their trade."],[2129.84,"And so what St."],[2130.64,"Paul is trying to say is I'm hey, I'm not doing that."],[2134.5,"That's that's the norm I know."],[2136.95,"You've had and there were there were apparently, because there's more references than that, there were people coming with Christian teachers coming and doing the same thing right coming and sort of expecting to be supported right while they were there."],[2154.11,"I've seen that that's yeah um I think we can all think of examples of people peddling the word of God yeah without having to name names um but uh but yeah so there are people he and St."],[2169.31,"Paul is saying I'm not doing that right I'm not doing that allll he just' saying uh everything that St."],[2176.43,"Paul is preaching is sincere."],[2178.39,"He means it, he believes it."],[2180.89,"He's staking his life on it over and over again, literally."],[2185.15,"Number two he, was sent by God to do it."],[2188.67,"He didn't just decide to do it."],[2190.19,"He didn't decide it would be a good racket."],[2191.96,"He didn't decide this would be a good way to make a living."],[2194.52,"God sent him to do it."],[2196.28,"And then finally, he says that being sent by God, he's now accountable to God."],[2205.46,"The side of God in Christ."],[2206.98,"He's the one who will judge whether St."],[2209.38,"Paul's been faithful in completing the mission that he was sent on."],[2215.51,"So, and and what connects those two in terms of his worthiness is right, the people who are traveling and sort of peddling their teaching."],[2223.45,"It's their teaching that they're peddling."],[2226.67,"St. Paul can't do that because it's not his. teaching"],[2230.71,"It's something that Christ gave him when Christ appeared to him and told him to go and take to these different places."],[2238.23,"So he can't sell it because it's not his."],[2258.75,"Others epistles of commendation to you or letters of commendation from you."],[2265.47,"Okay, so he's saying, Um, you know, do I have to lay out my credentials for you?"],[2273.63,"Obviously, they know him."],[2275.24,"So he's saying, I, you know, it's a rhetorical question."],[2278.5,"I don't need to do right."],[2279.86,"I don't need to bring you some letters of recommendation from someone else to tell you who I right, you know."],[2285.96,"You know who I am."],[2288.01,"And he also says, I don't need any from you either."],[2292.07,"I don't need you to vouch for me."],[2294.32,"I have, you know."],[2303.32,"Clearly, you are an epistle of Christ ministered by us, written not with ink, but by the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone, but on tablets of flesh, that is, of the heart."],[2315.2,"Paul is here talking about how he views them"],[2317.64,"So St. Remember, he just used that analogy of life and death that comes from the Torah."],[2326.36,"That's why he says not tablets of stone."],[2328.7,"Nothing had been written on tablets of stone for quite some time."],[2332.06,"At the point St."],[2332.94,"Paul is writing, he's referring back to that."],[2335.4,"And so, what is he saying about them by comparing them to an epistle, to a letter, a letter from God, a communicate written communication from God to the Torah itself?"],[2344.68,"Well, number one, he's saying, You, the community in Corinth."],[2349.08,"He's talking about how he views them."],[2351.38,"You're a gift of God to me."],[2357.48,"From everything he's just been through, that would not be how they would see themselves"],[2363.69,"He's had to go out of his way to say, I'm not mad at anyone there."],[2367.15,"I'm not grieved at anyone."],[2368.59,"I've forgiven everyone."],[2369.81,"There's no, but now he's going further than that."],[2372.77,"He's not like, hey man, I'm not worried about it."],[2375.03,"He's saying, no, I consider you a gift of God to me."],[2381.32,"Because he says that the Holy Spirit, he's comparing this to writing, but he's saying the Holy Spirit is active in all of their lives."],[2387.96,"And active in the life of the community."],[2391.72,"And things are happening there through which God is communicating to and teaching St."],[2397.0,"Paul. Not just them and their midst."],[2400.76,"And they're a communication, he says that's that's read by all"],[2406.52,"So how they live as a community and how they live as Christians, each of them and then them together."],[2413.45,"Is something that God is doing in their midst, and it's something that's being seen by the city of Corinth, by other Christian communities in other places."],[2423.71,"By the outside world."],[2426.49,"Because Corinth is a major hub, yes."],[2431.27,"And so this is how they need to be."],[2435.19,"He's again, he's reshaping how they look at themselves and look at their community."],[2440.12,"And what the what the goal needs to be."],[2443.42,"The goal is not, well, if we could just get rid of these troublemakers and get them all to leave, then we'd be at peace and everything would be fine."],[2451.2,"That's thinking way too small for what St."],[2455.52,"Paul is uh wants for them and and for why the gospel was given to them."],[2461.78,"That that uh that that they're being transformed, all the things that they're going through as they overcome them, as they reconcile to one another, as they experience God's love and show that love to one another, it's not just that these good things are happening to or with them, but they're being seen by everyone else."],[2480.9,"And so this is another way that the gospel is being communicated to the whole world is through this letter, not the letter we're reading, the letter that is the community in Corinth."],[2498.88,"That they communicate who Christ is to the whole world by how they live when they're following the Holy Spirit."],[2509.52,"That's both a statement."],[2511.29,"He's saying that's true, but that's also obviously a call."],[2516.63,"An aspiration. That's setting a bar."],[2518.73,"This is what we need to live up to."],[2521.63,"This is what we need to live up to."],[2523.39,"Because we're the letter to everyone else."],[2525.66,"And this is true of every Christian community."],[2527.1,"This is true here in Lafayette."],[2529.72,"This community here in this church is a letter from God."],[2535.96,"An epistle from God to the city of Lafayette, to Acadiana."],[2553.78,"Is showing who he is."],[2556.88,"To the world, to the world around us."],[2561.12,"And so, yes."],[2562.08,"So, in our case, too."],[2563.62,"It's true, and it's something we need to always continue to seek to live up to."],[2573.18,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2580.54,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2585.9,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":23.12,"lv":2,"h":"Chapter breaks; picking up at 1:23","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up <b>2 Corinthians 1:23</b> - left off at end of ch. 1</li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter break = start of a <b>new section</b></li><li class=\"d1\">chapters not added until the <b>medieval period</b> - placed \"haphazardly\"</li><li class=\"d1\">earliest MSS: <b>all capitals</b>, no punctuation, no spaces, left-right then right-left</li><li class=\"d1\">paragraph/sentence breaks <b>a little arbitrary</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes makes sense; sometimes \"move it back three verses\" - but now <b>standard</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No recap: Paul's <b>opening chapter lays out themes</b> he explores through the letter</li><li class=\"d1\">will see them come back as we go</li></ul>"},{"t":170.86,"lv":2,"h":"1:23-24 - Sparing them; not lording it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:23</b>: calls <b>God as witness</b> - came no more to Corinth <b>to spare you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">had planned to visit <b>twice</b>; didn't work out</li><li class=\"d1\">last visit <b>didn't go well</b> - not the <b>opportune time</b></li><li class=\"d1\">purpose of letter: <b>smooth things over</b>, calm things down</li><li class=\"d1\">not mad or holding a grudge - just <b>not helpful</b> to come in person yet</li><li class=\"d1\">\"This is absolutely true\" - not just making everybody feel better</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1:24</b>: not that we have <b>dominion</b> over your faith</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>dominion</i> related to <b>domination</b> - not coming to <b>force compliance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"faith\" better as <b>faithfulness</b> - loyalty, trust, not just intellectual belief (from Romans)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>fellow workers</b> with Timothy - \"you stand by your own faithfulness\"</li><li class=\"d1\">gives them <b>benefit of the doubt</b>: \"I'm not coming to lord it over you\"</li></ul>"},{"t":392.82,"lv":2,"h":"2:1-4 - Not another sorrowful visit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:1-2</b>: determined not to come <b>in sorrow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a little poetic\" - if I make you sorrowful, who makes <b>me</b> glad?</li><li class=\"d1\">more bad visits, strife → the one really sorrowed is <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ = the one who <b>comforts me in my sorrows</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's goal: <b>resolve</b>, not exacerbate - \"or even win a fight\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:3</b>: wrote this very thing - hoped for <b>joy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">refers to <b>1 Corinthians</b> - \"go look it up\"; not what happened</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:4</b>: wrote \"with many tears\" - not to grieve you but that you know my <b>love</b></li><li class=\"d1\">= the in-between <b>sorrowful letter</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sorrow was <b>brokenhearted</b>, not anger - \"coming from love\"</li></ul>"},{"t":551.52,"lv":2,"h":"2:5-8 - Don't be too severe","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:5</b>: if anyone caused grief - Paul has <b>forgiven</b> anything done to him</li><li class=\"d1\">means those who caused the community's problems</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor: man in <b>sexual immorality</b> with his <b>mother-in-law</b>, all knew, no one acted</li><li class=\"d1\">others involved in other problems too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I don't have anything against anybody\" → <b>sets the standard</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:6</b>: punishment by the <b>majority</b> is sufficient</li><li class=\"d1\">community took <b>disciplinary action</b>; probably more than one person</li><li class=\"d1\">enough - don't keep <b>ostracizing</b> or punishing forever</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>forgive and comfort</b>, lest he be <b>swallowed up</b> by sorrow</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:8</b>: reaffirm your <b>love</b> to him</li></ul>"},{"t":717.11,"lv":3,"h":"Discipline is for repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Excommunication / not receiving sacraments today - <b>never punishment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"you did this, we're mad\" or \"you hurt me, I'll hurt you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goal = bring to <b>repentance</b>, <b>reconcile</b>, restore</li><li class=\"d1\">if he won't acknowledge problems, act to say \"<b>come to your senses</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: I know you're hurt, angry - but can't <b>keep punishing</b> forever</li><li class=\"d1\">point was <b>correct things, restore peace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keep punishing → he's <b>swallowed up by sorrow</b>, never recovers</li></ul>"},{"t":811.89,"lv":2,"h":"2:8-11 - Forgiveness and Satan's devices","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:8-11</b>: test whether you are <b>obedient</b>; whom you forgive, I forgive</li><li class=\"d1\">reiterates: not holding anything against anyone</li><li class=\"d1\">forgiven \"<b>in the presence of Christ</b>\" = has <b>prayed</b> for them, that they'd be forgiven and restored</li><li class=\"d0\">Warning: <b>lest Satan take advantage</b> - \"not ignorant of his devices\"</li><li class=\"d1\">your hurt is <b>legitimate</b>, what they did was wrong, correction taken</li><li class=\"d1\">but devil uses hurt to make you fall into <b>bitterness, hatred, anger, revenge</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers talk about this a lot: not forgiving = <b>torturing ourselves</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other person often doesn't know or care - \"twisting up our own gut\"</li><li class=\"d1\">forgiveness not \"letting them off the hook\" - <b>freeing ourselves</b> from the passion</li><li class=\"d1\">free to move on, change, grow in Christ; people who spent whole lives <b>bitter and vengeful</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Danger to <b>both sides</b> - not just the troublemaker; don't want him driven to <b>despair</b>, or you destroyed too</li></ul>"},{"t":1057.16,"lv":2,"h":"2:12-13 - Troas and Macedonia","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:12-13</b>: at <b>Troas</b> door opened for the gospel - no rest, did not find <b>Titus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">left for <b>Macedonia</b> (Roman province) - also in <b>Acts</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Back to his <b>travel agenda</b> - why he didn't visit</li><li class=\"d1\">visiting then not best <b>and</b> new opportunity to preach opened</li><li class=\"d0\">Why so much detail? Must not think he's <b>avoiding</b> them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"He must be mad at us\" / mad at the troublemakers → \"keep beating them\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you drove Paul away too\" - another thing to be angry about</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul corrects that impression</li></ul>"},{"t":1163.99,"lv":2,"h":"2:14-16 - The Roman triumph","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:14-17</b> read as a unit - an <b>extended metaphor</b> not obvious in English or modern culture</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Leads us in triumph</b>\" - we hear \"we're victorious\"; actually a particular thing</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>triumph</b> = like a <b>parade</b> - end of WWII victory parades in New York, Washington</li><li class=\"d1\">official Roman honor for <b>great generals</b>, Caesar - not \"I'll celebrate me\"</li><li class=\"d1\">after great victory - literal military, or over a plague, adversity</li><li class=\"d0\">Not random: <i>evangelion</i> (<b>gospel</b>) used in the plural <i>evangelia</i> in the empire</li><li class=\"d1\">read out when a <b>victorious general or Caesar</b> was about to visit a city</li><li class=\"d1\">who they are + list of <b>great accomplishments</b>: \"he divided Gaul into three parts, killed a million people...\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so you could <b>prepare to receive him</b></li><li class=\"d0\">NT picks this word for apostles' proclaiming <b>Christ's victory</b> over sin, death, hell</li></ul>"},{"t":1410.69,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: \"good news\" as the meaning of gospel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: isn't <i>euangelion</i> just \"good news\"?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>woodenly literal</b> - <i>eu</i> = good, <i>angelion</i> = news or report</li><li class=\"d1\">bad way to interpret: pulling a word apart - <b>butterfly</b> has nothing to do with butter or fly</li><li class=\"d1\">not completely off, but vague - \"I saved money on car insurance\" is good news</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant \"what the gospel is\" = <b>how you get saved</b> (four spiritual laws tract)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God loves you and has a wonderful plan...\" - about <b>you</b> and what you do, not <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: \"gospel of <b>Jesus Christ</b>\" - not a self-help program</li></ul>"},{"t":1527.7,"lv":3,"h":"What the gospel is - Acts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">In <b>Acts</b> every time apostles preach, response: \"<b>What must I do to be saved?</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">if gospel = what to do, he'd know and not ask</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospel = <b>who Christ is and the victory he's won</b></li><li class=\"d1\">incarnation, death, burial, resurrection, ascension, right hand of God, <b>coming to judge</b> the living and dead</li><li class=\"d1\">response if you believed: \"what do I need to do?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So: <b>response</b> to the gospel is \"what do I need to do to be saved\" - not the gospel itself</li></ul>"},{"t":1602.91,"lv":2,"h":"Triumph in suffering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul extends metaphor: report of victory → Christ <b>leads us continually</b> in triumphal procession</li><li class=\"d0\">Interesting: last two chapters didn't sound triumphant</li><li class=\"d1\">sorrow, trouble, people grieved; struggling with <b>one church</b> - others have problems too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't sound much like a <b>victory parade</b>\" - Paul: that's what it is</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel: to unbelievers <b>crucifixion</b> didn't look like a victory</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ tortured to death on a Roman cross - \"not the image of a winner\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not just mindset to keep going - he's <b>reframing</b> for them</li><li class=\"d1\">they're reacting from hurt and sorrow → forgive, reconcile, show love</li><li class=\"d1\">see it like the <b>crucifixion</b> (sharing Christ's sufferings, ch. 1)</li><li class=\"d1\">run out of town, half stoned, beaten, flogged = a <b>victory march</b>, even if outsiders don't see it</li></ul>"},{"t":1801.83,"lv":2,"h":"Fragrance: life to life, death to death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fragrance of his knowledge</b> = the report of the gospel, <b>knowledge of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">compared to <b>incense</b> used in processions - offerings going on</li><li class=\"d1\">knowledge of Christ spreads like incense as he travels</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:15-16</b>: fragrance of Christ to those <b>being saved</b> and those <b>perishing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">believers, faithful, following vs. those rejecting it</li><li class=\"d0\">Two kinds of people in a Roman triumph</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>participants</b>: fellow soldiers, celebrants - smell = <b>life and victory</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>captured prisoners and slaves</b>: end of procession = \"the end of them\" - paraded, then <b>done in</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unbelievers see only <b>shame of the crucifixion</b>, shame of Paul → \"foul smell\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Same message</b>, same report - life or death by how it's received</li></ul>"},{"t":1983.15,"lv":3,"h":"Deuteronomy: life and death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Scriptural background for \"life and death\" - Corinth mostly <b>Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deut</b>: \"Behold, I set before you today <b>life and death</b>, blessings and curses\"</li><li class=\"d1\">right at end of the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Yahweh: I've given you Torah - depending on how you <b>receive</b> it = life or death</li><li class=\"d1\">same with the gospel as Paul proclaims it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Who is <b>sufficient</b> for these things?\" - not bragging</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not his triumph - Christ's</b>; not worthy to be the one doing this</li></ul>"},{"t":2062.05,"lv":2,"h":"2:17 - Not peddling the word of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>2:17</b>: not as so many <b>peddling</b> the word of God</li><li class=\"d1\">= not doing this for <b>money</b>; he refers to it in <b>1 Corinthians</b> - \"supported myself with my own hands\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient norm: philosophers, teachers, <b>rabbis</b> paid by place they came to</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbi stays at a synagogue, supported until he moves on</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Simon Magus</b> in Acts tried to buy ability to give the Spirit - \"teach me this technique\"</li><li class=\"d1\">apparently <b>Christian teachers</b> doing the same - \"we can all think of examples\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's three points</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sincere</b> - means it, \"staking his life on it over and over, literally\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sent by God</b> - not \"a good racket\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>accountable to God</b> - \"in the sight of God in Christ\"; he judges faithfulness to the mission</li><li class=\"d0\">Can't sell it: <b>not his teaching</b> - Christ gave it when he appeared to him</li></ul>"},{"t":2258.75,"lv":2,"h":"3:1-3 - You are our epistle","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1</b>: need <b>letters of commendation</b> to you or from you?</li><li class=\"d1\">rhetorical - they know him; no letters of recommendation needed either way</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:2-3</b>: you are an <b>epistle of Christ</b>, written not with ink but by the <b>Spirit of the living God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not on <b>tablets of stone</b>, but tablets of flesh, of the heart\" - back to the <b>Torah</b>, life/death imagery</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing written on stone tablets for a long time by then</li><li class=\"d0\">Comparing them to a <b>letter from God</b>, the Torah itself</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>#1</b>: how he views them - a <b>gift of God to me</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not how they'd see themselves; beyond \"I'm not mad, I've forgiven everyone\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Holy Spirit active in each life and the community - God <b>teaching Paul</b> through them</li></ul>"},{"t":2400.76,"lv":3,"h":"The community as the letter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A communication <b>\"read by all\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how they live as Christians, individually and together = what God is doing</li><li class=\"d1\">seen by <b>city of Corinth</b>, other Christian communities, outside world - Corinth a major <b>hub</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reshaping their goal</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"get rid of the troublemakers, then peace\" - \"thinking way too small\"</li><li class=\"d1\">being <b>transformed</b>: overcoming, reconciling, experiencing and showing God's love - <b>seen by everyone</b></li><li class=\"d1\">another way the gospel is communicated: the <b>letter that is the community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Both a <b>statement</b> and a <b>call</b> - aspiration, \"setting a bar\"</li><li class=\"d1\">true of every community, <b>Lafayette</b>: this church = letter from God to the city, <b>Acadiana</b>, showing who he is</li><li class=\"d1\">\"something we need to always continue to seek to live up to\"</li></ul>"}]});
